# Author identities, consolidated for display only.
#
# Git stores whatever `user.email` was configured at the time, and this
# repository carries three addresses for one person. This file maps them to one
# identity so `git log`, `git shortlog` and `git blame` report contributors
# truthfully. Nothing is rewritten: every commit keeps the address it was
# authored with, and every hash stays what it was.
#
# The corporate address is the one worth explaining. It was the configured
# `user.email` from the first commit on 2026-06-16 until it was changed on
# 2026-07-25, and it covers 88 commits — the same person, the same `user.name`,
# a global Git setting inherited from another machine's projects. It says
# nothing about who owns the work; that question belongs to an employment
# agreement and is recorded in NOTICE and in this branch's task file.
#
# Note what this does not reach: GitHub's web interface ignores .mailmap, so
# commit pages there still show the address each commit was made with.

maxim.berenshtein <shteynumaks@gmail.com> <maxim.berenshtein@zoominfo.com>
maxim.berenshtein <shteynumaks@gmail.com> Max <40126999+shteynu@users.noreply.github.com>
